.TH AEROBRAKINGTEST 1
.SH NAME
aerobrakingtest \- test aerobraking's eqn-driven rhs() against hand-computed values
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aerobrakingtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Aerobrakingtest
checks
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.BR rhs() " and " observables() ,
now written as literal
.IR eqn (7)
strings rather than hand-coded Limbo arithmetic (see
.IR eqn2hoc (2)),
against values computed independently in Python before this file was
written, for one representative in-atmosphere craft state.
.PP
Checks the central 1/r\(ha3 gravity term, the atmospheric drag term
(density, then a shared drag factor, then two components - the same
chain
.IR danbyeqnall (1)
already verified against
.BR atmosphere->drag2() ),
and that the "planet" body's own four state components stay fixed at
zero (this model's
.B Orbital
state array carries a planet body that never moves, despite Orbital
being the array/N-body family elsewhere - see
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own SOURCE section).
.PP
Deliberately does
.B not
compare against a second, separately loaded Orbital instance in the
same process - see BUGS.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/aerobrakingtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby-aerobraking (1),
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
.IR orbital (2),
.IR danbyeqnall (1)
.SH BUGS
An early version of this test loaded the eqn-driven
.B aerobraking.dis
.B and
a byte-for-byte copy of the original hand-written Limbo implementation
into the SAME process, to compare
.B Model.rhs()
directly - the same oracle-comparison pattern
.IR danbyeqnall (1)
uses successfully for Trajectory. It gave a silent FALSE PASS on a
deliberately broken version: Limbo's
.RI ( self " ref " Model )
dot-method dispatch on an
.I adt
resolves through whichever module a caller's own
.RB \*(lq "Model: import" "\*(rq"
names at compile time, not dynamically per the object's actual
.I origin
\(em calling
.B .rhs
on a
.B Model
built by a
.I second
loaded instance silently ran the
.I first
instance's compiled function instead, operating on the second
instance's own data. Confirmed directly: the same model, loaded and
called in ISOLATION, gave the correct oracle value; loaded ALONGSIDE a
second instance with one shared import, both sides silently returned
the
.I same
(first instance's) value, including on a version with an injected
sign-flip bug - a comparison that looked clean while comparing nothing
at all. Ordinary module-function calls
.RI ( m -> foo() ,
not a
.I self
method) dispatch correctly per instance; only the
.I adt
self-method sugar is affected. This test avoids the pitfall by never
loading two
.B Orbital
instances in the same process; a future test wanting a live
Limbo-to-Limbo oracle comparison for a
.I self
method needs two separate process invocations, compared from outside
(shell-level), not two loads in one process.
